//sha-256;sQgfB9A0-0UKvf_yZevAAwNkqiB4OL5x1r5RNL3qQ2Qƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyOutside a public library in Santa Ana, California, sat a bronze statue of a man with a book under one arm, a dove taking flight from the other. Palestinian-American poet, scholar, and civil rights leader Alex Odeh was assassinated in 1985; nine years later, Algerian-American artist Khalil Bendib made the statue in his honor. It was vandalized with red paint in 1996 and then again the year after; in 2020, the dove â€” a symbol of peace â€” was stolen, before eventually being recovered. Now, artist Jon Rubin is setting that dove free, sending it on a world tour as a living, participatory artwork that brings renewed attention to Odehâ€™s life and legacy, a continued history of anti-Arab violence, and efforts to end occupation and genocide in Palestine â€” a quiet prayer for peace. This past weekend, it flew to Los Angeles for an evening of music, poetry, and food; next, itâ€™ll head to the Bay Area, Pittsburgh, and hopefully, to Jifna, Odehâ€™s hometown, before returning home to roost. Itâ€™s a timely reminder that art is something meant to be shared in community â€” as Patrick Guetta, the artist featured in this weekâ€™s edition of A View From the Easel, puts it, â€œOne of my favorite museums is the street.â€ Also today, inside the cheerfully sinister worlds of Trevor Winkfield, and Zendaya wearing 3,000-year-old Iranian artifacts to a movie premiere points to bigger problems than out-of-touch celebrities. â€”Lisa Yin Zhang, associate editor CORRECTION: Our Daily Newsletter from Friday, July 17, mistakenly stated that three antiquities the Manhattan District Attorneyâ€™s Office returned to Mexico were previously held at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, when in fact only one of them was. We regret the error. A Living Memorial to Palestinian Activist Alex Odeh Takes Flight â€œThe Stolen Doveâ€ is a participa